/**

@page benchmarks_exagrype_single_black_hole Single black hole (ExaGRyPE Python API)

A single Schwarzschild black hole at the origin of the domain
\f$ [-9,9]^3 \f$. The initial data stems from TwoPunctures. The simulation
should settle into a (quasi-)stationary state after a while, but it is
numerically challenging: there is a singularity at the puncture, and the
boundary of the domain is not physical.

This benchmark sets up the black hole with exagrype.api.CCZ4Solver and
discretises it with the Finite Volume scheme of exahype2.api on a regular
mesh. It mirrors the pure Finite Volume runs of the legacy benchmark
@ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole. The tutorial
@ref tutorials_exahype2_exagrype_522_single-black-hole explains how the
benchmark is built step by step.

# Build and run

Configure Peano with

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exagrype --with-gsl ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

TwoPunctures needs the GNU Scientific Library (GSL). Without --with-gsl, the
generated solver refuses to compile. Build Peano, then run, within this
directory,

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 single-black-hole.py
./single-black-hole
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The script builds a small debug version by default: \f$ 9^3 \f$ mesh cells,
each carrying a patch of \f$ 6^3 \f$ volumes, run until t=0.1. It takes
around 2.5 minutes on an eight-core workstation. Set \c create_release_build
to True for an optimised build on the same mesh which runs until t=10. It
takes around 230 time steps of 5 seconds each. Every time you make the mesh
cells three times smaller (cells_per_axis), the run becomes 81 times more
expensive: 27 times more volumes and three times more time steps.

# What to expect

The initial data matches the closed form of a Schwarzschild black hole in
isotropic coordinates up to round-off. The run is stable, but the mesh is far
too coarse for the black hole: the horizon spans about three volumes, and the
first-order Finite Volumes diffuse the puncture away. By t=10, the lapse next
to the puncture has grown from 0.37 to 0.89, and the conformal factor phi
from 0.13 to 0.93. Without constraint enforcement, the numbers are almost the
same (0.88 and 0.93), i.e. the enforcement does not cure the dissolving black
hole. A resolved black hole would instead show a collapsing
lapse. The tutorial @ref tutorials_exahype2_exagrype_522_single-black-hole
discusses this behaviour.

# Differences to the legacy benchmark

- The legacy code imposes Sommerfeld boundary conditions. This benchmark
  uses Dirichlet conditions instead, which hold the boundary at the
  (static) initial data.
- The mesh is regular. The legacy code zooms into the black hole with
  adaptive mesh refinement, and couples the Finite Volumes with fourth-order
  Finite Differences.

*/
